From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 18 Aug 2026 00:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] sdhost: do not name ARM instructions in a portable
 backend

Third of its kind, after 0013 (usb2otg) and 0025 (dma.resource), and the same
one-line shape: a barrier written as inline AArch64 in a driver that is
otherwise portable.

    static inline void sdhost_dsb(void) { asm volatile("dsb sy" ::: "memory"); }

Everything else in this file already is portable -- MMIO goes through
AROS_LE2LONG/AROS_LONG2LE, the peripheral base comes from
KrnGetSystemAttr(KATTR_PeripheralBase), and the NEON bounce copy directly
above guards itself on __arm__ and falls back to a word loop.

What makes this one worth a message rather than a diff is how it fails
elsewhere. On m68k, gas parses "dsb" as the MRI-style define-storage-bytes
directive, so `dsb sy` assembles as a .space of an undefined symbol:

    Error: .space specifies non-absolute value

reported at whatever line the deferred expression is finally resolved on --
an ordinary instruction, nowhere near the asm, in a file containing no .space
at all. sdhost_dsb() is inlined at seven sites, so the error count tracks the
inline sites and moves with -O level, which reads like an optimiser bug and is
not one.

<asm/cpu.h> is where every ARM target already gets dsb(), and this port
supplies its own (added for the USB driver in 0013). Using it changes nothing
on ARM -- arch/arm-all's dsb() is that instruction -- and gives m68k the
honest answer: a compiler barrier, because the guest has no barrier
instruction to issue and what reaches the bus is AArch64 the JIT emitted.

Upstream would take this. Naming the instruction was the accident, and every
ARM target keeps exactly the semantics it has today.

See AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0013.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../soc/broadcom/2708/sdcard/sdcard_sdhost_bus.c   | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/sdcard/sdcard_sdhost_bus.c b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/sdcard/sdcard_sdhost_bus.c
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/sdcard/sdcard_sdhost_bus.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/sdcard/sdcard_sdhost_bus.c
@@ -36,6 +36,8 @@
 #include <proto/exec.h>
 #include <proto/kernel.h>
 #include <proto/dma.h>
+
+#include <asm/cpu.h>
 #include <proto/utility.h>
 
 #include <hardware/mmc.h>
@@ -57,7 +59,24 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
-static inline void sdhost_dsb(void) { asm volatile("dsb sy" ::: "memory"); }
+/*
+ * The barrier every register write below is ordered against.
+ *
+ * Spelled through <asm/cpu.h> rather than as inline AArch64, because naming
+ * the instruction here makes this file ARM-only for no reason -- the rest of
+ * it is portable, and the NEON copy above already guards itself on __arm__.
+ *
+ * On m68k the misspelling is not even a build failure that points at itself:
+ * gas parses "dsb" as the MRI-style define-storage-bytes directive, so
+ * `dsb sy` becomes a .space of an undefined symbol and the diagnostic reads
+ *
+ *     Error: .space specifies non-absolute value
+ *
+ * at whatever line the deferred expression is finally resolved on. This is an
+ * inline function used seven times, so the error count tracks the inline sites
+ * rather than the mistake.
+ */
+static inline void sdhost_dsb(void) { dsb(); }
 
